+++From 3d1541eefd935d66be9fb4e2597cd1030cd8120a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
+++Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 06:03:23 -0700
+++Subject: [PATCH 05/16] promscrape: stop decompressing the whole response
+++ before stream parsing
+++
+++scrapeInternal decompressed the entire response into memory before
+++deciding whether to stream-parse it, so stream parsing only saved the
+++parsed rows while the full decompressed response stayed resident: a
+++higress with 30k keys is 67 MiB compressed and 1.5 GiB decompressed, so
+++three targets scraped at once start at 4.5 GiB.
+++
+++The full decompressed response is only needed for staleness markers and
+++series_limit, both of which compare against the previous response. With
+++staleness markers disabled and no series_limit configured, the compressed
+++response is now handed to the stream parser directly, decompressed and
+++parsed in chunks and pushed to the aggregator as it goes, with the
+++response size counted while reading. Whether to stream is still decided by
+++-promscrape.minResponseSizeForStreamParse; since the decompressed size is
+++unknown beforehand, the previous response size and the bytes read so far
+++are used instead.
+++
+++Stream reading treats io.ErrUnexpectedEOF as a normal end of input. Here
+++the response has already been read into memory in full, so seeing it means
+++the compressed data is corrupt; it is turned into an ordinary error and
+++fails the scrape, as whole-response decompression does. (A truncated zstd
+++frame returns io.EOF on both paths, which is existing behaviour.)
+++
+++Measured on a real 30k-key response, three targets scraped at once with
+++the production aggregation config: vmagent peak RSS 11.6 GiB -> 4.2 GiB
+++with identical sample counts, and 0.88 GiB with aggregation off. Hashing
+++all 4,415,000 samples in-process gives identical results, and a single
+++parse goes from 15.5s to 1.2s.
