++@@ -0,0 +1,327 @@
+++From de22abbb4577297c36fcbeeadbdacbe1605c11de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
+++Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 04:35:05 -0700
+++Subject: [PATCH 10/16] promutil, streamaggr: cache the most recent label
+++ lookups
+++
+++Compressing the labels of a sample looks every label up in a shared
+++sync.Map, which was 46% of the stream aggregation push profile. The labels
+++of a scrape repeat heavily -- job, instance, namespace, the metric name
+++and any low-cardinality dimension are identical across huge numbers of
+++series -- so most of those lookups ask the same question again.
+++
+++CompressCached answers from a small direct-mapped cache in front of the
+++map, one per push context and therefore one per goroutine, so it needs no
+++synchronisation of its own. A hit compares the name and value against the
+++entry the slot holds; anything else falls through to the shared map and
+++refills the slot. What the slot keeps is the interned copy the compressor
+++already holds, never the caller's buffer, so a cached entry cannot outlive
+++its strings.
+++
+++It is safe because an index, once assigned to a label, never changes:
+++LoadOrStore resolves the race between two goroutines interning the same
+++label and every caller ends up with the winner.
+++
+++Both paths that compress labels use it: the aggregator and the
+++deduplicator, which an aggregation config reaches through dedup_interval.
+++
+++Measured, alternating the versions in one tree and taking medians:
+++
+++  BenchmarkAggregatorsPush/output=total
+++    before the sorting change  202.9 ms/op
+++    with sorting               179.4 ms/op   -11.6%
+++    with sorting and this      147.4 ms/op   -27.4%
+++
+++  BenchmarkDeduplicatorPush
+++    without this               1.068 ms/op
+++    with this                  0.775 ms/op   -27.4%
+++
+++Two cheaper hashes were tried and both lost: hash/maphash over the label
+++struct came out 2.6% slower, and hashing only the ends of a long value
+++7.5% slower, because the weaker hash collides more and every extra miss
+++costs a shared-map lookup, which is far more than the hashing it saved.
+++Hash quality matters more than hash cost here.
+++
+++TestCompressCachedMatchesCompress compares the output against the
+++uncached path over sixty thousand random label sets drawn from more
+++distinct labels than the cache has slots, so entries are evicted and
+++reused, including the empty label that an unused slot looks like.
+++TestCompressCachedSlotCollision builds two labels that hash to the same
+++slot and share a value, where only the name tells them apart.
+++
+++The random test alone did not catch dropping the name comparison: with
+++values unique per label, no collision can produce a wrong hit. The
+++collision test exists because of that.
