+++From 08f4b377d894f1d3b0b0cfe79ed281382a4fc530 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
+++Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 10:41:40 -0700
+++Subject: [PATCH 15/16] lib/streamaggr: build the input part of a key only when
+++ an output reads it
+++
+++A sample's key is the output part -- what the aggregation groups by -- followed
+++by the input part, the labels it groups away. The input part exists for outputs
+++that keep state per input series: total, increase, rate_avg, rate_sum and
+++count_series. The other eleven take the sample and nothing else; their
+++pushSample signatures say so by taking the key as _.
+++
+++Building it anyway costs a hash and two string compares per label per sample,
+++for a `without: [pod, instance, node]` rule three of about eleven labels, plus
+++the length in every key the state map holds and hashes.
+++
+++useInputKey already existed and getInputOutputKey already handled its being
+++off, but it was set from dedup alone, and compressLabels never looked at it. It
+++is now narrowed to what the configured outputs answer, and compressLabels
+++leaves the input part out when nothing will read it.
+++
+++BenchmarkAggregatorsPush/output=sum_samples_total, run on its own:
+++
+++  before  119.9ms/op   8.34 MB/s  13030 B/op  40 allocs/op
+++  after    69.7ms/op  14.35 MB/s   6332 B/op  19 allocs/op
+++
+++Run on its own because this package's benchmarks share one LabelsCompressor:
+++in a combined run total reported 84955 B/op against 1760197 B/op alone, warmed
+++by whatever ran before it. total is unaffected by this change either way, as it
+++needs the input key and keeps it.
+++
+++needsInputKey is on the aggrConfig interface so that a new output has to answer
+++it, next to the pushSample whose signature is the answer. Getting it wrong does
+++not fail: count_series would see every input as the same one and count 1. The
+++tests pin each output's answer against its signature and the union rule across
+++several outputs; removing one output's true makes them fail, and ten existing
+++tests in this package fail with it.
