+++From 5d55c7d331b6a26d4e189c52303c5a7736ab8383 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+++From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
+++Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 17:15:42 -0700
+++Subject: [PATCH 16/16] lib/promutil: check the hand-written label sort against
+++ the standard library
+++
+++Sort is an insertion sort straight on the slice rather than a call through
+++sort.Interface, and nothing checked that it orders the same way. It sits on
+++the stream aggregation push path, where a wrong order does not fail: it
+++changes the key a sample groups under, so samples land in the wrong output
+++series and the numbers are quietly wrong.
+++
+++Twenty thousand random label sets are compared against sort.SliceStable, drawn
+++from names that hit the edges -- shared prefixes, one name extending another,
+++names differing only in their tail, mixed case and underscores. Reversing the
+++comparison in Sort fails it on the first round.
+++
+++This was written while trying to make Sort compare a packed eight-byte prefix
+++of each name before the name itself, to get out of the string comparisons that
+++were 16.6% of the aggregation push profile. That lost and is not here: against
+++BenchmarkLabelsSortExposerOrdering, which models what an exposer actually
+++emits, it ran 41% slower with the generation label appended and 83% slower on
+++an already sorted set -- packing the keys costs more than the comparisons it
+++saves when the set is short and nearly ordered. It gained 6.6% on the upstream
+++push benchmark, whose synthetic label order is far more scrambled, while
+++allocating 45 times as much. The test is worth keeping either way.
